# KS-947 R16-F3F4b - REBRIEF 1 of 1 (Kam's 2026-09-16 counter) - brief defect: backslash-escaped quotes - test-only PIN of the KS-733 parity gate's two blind spots (F3 option set + mount order, F4 spec binding) at develop 64ab10513 (rebrief written 2026-09-22 by Wednesday's KS-947 rebrief drafter from `KS-947-R16-F3F4.md` (golden PASS 8/8, model FAIL T4 in `runs/2026-09-22_ks947-ornith35b-night`), the run's `checker.out` / `out.md.raw.json`, and ks733-users-mfa-rate-limit-mount.test.ts :29-99 at the tip)
File: `Blockchain/Dev/services/api-gateway/src/__tests__/ks733-users-mfa-rate-limit-mount.test.ts`
Tip: `64ab105132eada0621622acf4d6053bc59926780`
Runner: `vitest`

Rebrief note (what changed and why - the intent, the six cells, the three tampers and their reds are IDENTICAL to the first brief):
- The first brief's `+` lines carried NO backslash (grep over its `+` lines for a backslash-quote pair: 0). The checker's `MISSING '+' 'const blanked = call.replace(/\'[^\']*\'/g, ...'` line is Python repr quoting, not the brief's bytes. What the model emitted (`out.md.raw.json`) was the double quotes of `"''"` escaped as `\"''\"` on the ONE `+` line that mixed a quote-laden regex literal (`/'[^']*'/g`) with a double-quoted string - the model normalised the quoting and the byte-for-byte T4 predicate missed the line; the other 61 `+` lines were present byte-exact.
- Rule applied here: every `+` line is SINGLE-QUOTE-ONLY - no double quote character and no backslash anywhere in a `+` line, so there is nothing for the model to escape. The single-quote character the blanking needs is built from its char code (`String.fromCharCode(39)`, the same idiom as `LF = String.fromCharCode(10)`, which the model reproduced byte-exact), the string-literal matcher is a `new RegExp(...)` from that character, and the order control locates the users mount by the text `mountCall` already extracted instead of a needle that mixed the two quote kinds. Hunk grows 62 -> 66 `+` lines (`+96,66` -> `+96,70`).

## Premises (measured by the feed3 drafter at 01:58:37 AEST in a `--shared` scratchpad clone detached at 64ab10513, re-read by the rebrief drafter at the same tip; Wednesday re-derives before queueing)
- Ticket KS-947 (P2, Backlog, kamil.kreiser@secuura.ai, no PR attachment): "the parity cell compares only windowMs and max ... and is purely relative". Both fix shapes are the ticket's own: (F3) compare the WHOLE option set of the users mount against the auth mount and assert the users mount precedes `app.use(createProxyRoutes`; (F4) bind the spec's `x-ratelimit-*` to the mount values. Nothing in the product changes - the mounts are right at the tip; this task pins them.
- The test file reads `index.ts` as TEXT (`GATEWAY_SRC`, :33) and extracts a mount by balanced parentheses (`mountCall`, :43-58, returning the `app.use('<path>', ...)` call text sliced out of `GATEWAY_SRC`, or null) and its `windowMs`/`max` text (`limiterShape`, :61-65). The new cells reuse those three file-scope names and add nothing at file scope: every new declaration sits INSIDE the existing describe, after its last cell.
- At the tip: the auth mount is index.ts:991-996 (`app.use('/api/auth/mfa', rateLimit({` ... `max: isNonProd ? 100 : 10,` at :993, `standardHeaders: true, legacyHeaders: false,` at :995); the users mount is :1016-1021 (same body: :1018 max, :1020 standardHeaders); `app.use(createProxyRoutes({ services, authenticateToken, log }));` is :1094 - the users mount PRECEDES it. The spec `docs/openapi/secuura-api.yaml` carries exactly 9 `x-ratelimit-policy: mfa` lines, each followed by `x-ratelimit-window-ms: 900000` then `x-ratelimit-max: 10` (control: `x-ratelimit-policy: global` blocks carry 60000/300). `15 * 60 * 1000` = 900000 and the production arm of `isNonProd ? 100 : 10` is 10, so every F4 cell is GREEN at the tip.
- Reachability of each tamper from the declared cells: F3SKIP appends `skip: () => true,` to the users mount's :1020 - `optionKeys` reads that line's keys, so the F3 option-set cell reds (`extra` = ['skip']); the existing PARITY cell stays green (windowMs/max untouched). F4AUTH changes the auth mount's :993 production max 10 -> 5000: the F4-auth cell reds (spec says 10) AND the existing PARITY cell reds (users max text no longer equals auth max text). F4USERS does the same to the users mount's :1018: the F4-users cell and PARITY red. The F3 ORDER cell is a CONTROL: a mount MOVE is two edits and no one-site tamper expresses it, so it is pinned green on both trees and stated as such.
- Golden precheck fix carried over from the first brief: a `// TAMPER: ...` comment reads as an option key `TAMPER`, so `optionKeys` drops `//` line comments (a real mount comment would otherwise false-red the cell) and the three tamper comments carry no colon. The blanking of string literals happens BEFORE the comment drop, as in the first brief (a mount path never contains `//`, so the order is immaterial at the tip and kept for fidelity).
- The fence is ASCII-only, backslash-free AND double-quote-free in every `+` line (LF and the quote character are `String.fromCharCode`, character classes and quote pairs never sit inside a regex literal) - the KS-887 / KS-1133 escape rule plus the 2026-09-22 KS-947 mixed-quote rule. No blank `+` line and no blank context line (the 09-18 06:2x rule); the insertion ends with trailing context (:99 `});`).

## What is wrong (one paragraph)
`ks733-users-mfa-rate-limit-mount.test.ts` pins that `/api/users/me/mfa` carries a limiter with the SAME `windowMs`/`max` as `/api/auth/mfa` - and only that. Two behaviour-destroying edits keep it 4/4 green: adding `skip: () => true` to the users mount (no 429 at all, keys not compared) and moving the mount below `app.use(createProxyRoutes` (a limiter registered after the proxy never sees the request); and because the cell is purely relative, both mounts can drift to `max: 5000` while the spec advertises `x-ratelimit-max: 10` on nine MFA operations. This task is the PIN, test-only: six new cells appended inside the existing describe - an option-set cell (red under F3SKIP), an order control, a spec-walk control, one spec-binding cell per mount (each red under its own max tamper, alongside the existing PARITY cell), and a window-binding control. The product is untouched.

## THE MODE - read this twice
TEST-ONLY, MODIFY IN PLACE: your diff contains EXACTLY ONE file, the EXISTING test file above, as the ONE hunk below (context lines byte-for-byte from the tip; no blank context line; no blank `+` line; the `+` lines end with the trailing context `});` at :99). No product hunk. The hunk header is `@@ -96,4 +96,70 @@` - copy it. Every `+` line uses ONLY the single-quote character for strings - never write a double quote and never write a backslash in a `+` line; copy each line exactly as it stands.

## The exact change
